// @8bitscript/cx16/pointer — the X16 behind @8bitscript/pointer, and the
// machine where the gap is most embarrassing and most easily closed.
//
// Every call here does nothing and `DRAWS` is false, on the machine that
// has **more to draw a cursor with than any of the other eight**: VERA
// offers 128 sprites, and the X16's KERNAL has a mouse pointer built in —
// `$FF68` mouse_config turns one on and the firmware draws and moves it
// for you, which is the one machine of the nine where a program need not
// draw a cursor at all.
//
// It is false anyway, because reaching any of that means an `asm6502`
// block to make a KERNAL call, and @8bitscript/cx16/input is in exactly
// the same position for exactly the same reason: it names `$FFE4` GETIN,
// `$FF56` joystick_get and `$FF6B` mouse_get and cannot reach one of them.
// **The X16 is currently the machine that cannot be driven at all**, and a
// cursor is downstream of that: an arrow with nothing moving it is worse
// than no arrow.
//
// So the order of work is the input layer's three KERNAL calls first, and
// then this file has a choice the others do not — let the firmware draw
// the pointer (`mouse_config`, and `DRAWS` true with `update()` doing
// nothing at all, since the KERNAL moves it), or ignore it and drive a
// VERA sprite the way @8bitscript/c64/pointer drives a VIC one. The
// firmware's is almost certainly right: it is already written, it costs
// no sprite, and the X16's own software expects that pointer.
//
// It costs the X16 nothing today: `DRAWS` is a const and every function is
// empty, so a program's `if (pointer.DRAWS)` folds away and LLVM deletes
// the calls — the same zero this package's input layer measures at (see
// packages/input/AGENTS.md).
export namespace pointer {

    const DRAWS: bool = false;

    function begin(): void {
    }

    function setColor(color: utinyint): void {
    }

    function update(): void {
    }

    function hide(): void {
    }
}
